$ enola help flows
exit: 2
--- stdout
--- stderr
Usage: enola flows [flags] [repo_path|config_path]

Rank the ways into a program by what each reaches. An entry is a served
route's handler, a program's main, or a symbol a framework invokes; its flow
is every symbol the calls from it reach. With no flags it prints the largest
flows, one line each.

A program reached through one main is one flow. Its subcommands are steps of
that flow, shown by --show, and so is a handler written as a function literal,
which has no symbol of its own. A library has no entry and lists nothing.

A flow is a floor, not a measurement: the walk ends at any call whose target
is no symbol in the graph. The unresolved column counts those calls. Most of
them leave the repository, for the standard library or an imported package;
the rest are calls on a value whose type the extractor could not name.
Entries that reach the same symbols are folded into one flow, and a flow that
sits almost wholly inside a larger one says which.

The program's flows are listed first. A flow whose entry is an example, a
script, a migration or build tooling is marked side and listed after them,
however much it reaches.

Exit codes:
  0  the report was produced, including one that found no flows
  2  the command could not run, or --show or --through named nothing it
     could pick

Flags:
  -json
    	emit the report as JSON, every listed flow with its stages and summary
  -show string
    	describe one flow: its stages, what it reaches and a summary. Takes a flow's name or a part of it that only one has
  -through string
    	list only the flows that reach this symbol
  -top int
    	how many flows to list (1-500) (default 10)
